  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 238 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The surface high that brought the lower humidity and \nsunshine today will gradually retreat to the east going into the day \ntomorrow, as a low will attempt to push a warm front through. \nHowever, it will fail to fully gain entry into the region and a \nstationary boundary will end up getting stationed over or just south \nof the region into Wednesday. \n\nAn area of steady rain will push into the region late tonight and \ntomorrow morning, but most areas should just see an area of \nrelatively light stratiform precipitation and only pick up a couple \ntenths of an inch, with the Northeast Kingdom possibly remaining \ncompletely dry. However, an area of elevated convection may just \nclip southern Vermont and St. Lawrence County and could lead to \nlocally higher totals of 1-1.5 inches. A few rumbles of thunder are \npossible in those areas as well. However, it should occur over \nseveral hours and the threat for flash flooding is very low. The \nbest synoptic forcing exits in the morning and despite the boundary \nremaining around, there should only be a couple scattered showers \ngoing through the day, but it will likely be dreary.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A more dynamic low pushes through Wednesday night into \nThursday morning, bringing rounds of heavy rain. This one currently \nlooks to make it just far enough northeast that the center passes \nthrough. Therefore, the associated elevated convection looks to be \nable to enter the region and bring periods of heavy rain and \nembedded thunderstorms. However, due to the temperature profiles and \nthe nighttime occurrence, everything should remain elevated and not \nbe severe. It will be continued to be watched if the dynamics from \nthe storm would be strong enough to cause a couple storms with hail \nor gusty winds to break the inversion at the surface. Currently, \nmost areas should receive around an inch of rain, but convective \nenhancement will cause locally higher totals. GEFS/EPS/CMCE \nprobabilities of receiving more than an inch range between 25-50 \npercent and the probabilities of 2 inches are below 10 percent, but \nthe convective enhancement is not taken into account in those. The \nflood threat is low at this point. The one thing that will continued \nto be watched if the axis of heavy convective rain lines up with the \naxis tonight, but even then there will be multiple days in between \nand marginal totals.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: An upper low will swing across eastern Maine \nFriday/Friday night, bringing yet another round of showers, \nespecially over the northern high terrain. This feature will not \nmake much eastward progress over the weekend, keeping us under \ncyclonic flow. There may be just enough moisture wrapping back into \nthe region to keep the threat of showers around, especially in the \nafternoons, but don't anticipate a washout by any means. \nTemperatures may be a little on the cooler side, especially on the \nhigher summits, but this will depend on how much eastward progress \nthe upper low is able to make. Have stayed with the NBM/WPC forecast \nfor now since we're a ways out, but adjustments may need to be made \nas the weekend draws closer.",
